We fight monsters left to right. At each index $i$, there's a bonus $b_i$ (sum of boost values covering $i$). We need current strength $s + b_i \ge monsters[i]$, i.e., $s \ge monsters[i] - b_i$. After defeating, $s = \max(0, s - monsters[i])$.

We want minimum initial strength.

Let's think backwards. Let $f(i)$ = minimum strength needed just before fighting monster $i$ (to defeat monsters $i..n-1$). Then $f(n) = 0$ (no more monsters needed — actually after last monster, strength can be anything; we need 0 more).

For monster $i$: we need $s \ge monsters[i] - b_i$ and after fight $s' = \max(0, s - monsters[i]) \ge f(i+1)$. To have $\max(0, s - monsters[i]) \ge f(i+1)$, we need $s \ge monsters[i] + f(i+1)$ (if $f(i+1) > 0$; if $f(i+1) = 0$, then any $s$ works for this part, since $\max(0, s - monsters[i]) \ge 0$ always).

